WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.836
- Some of the things that
I like to remind people,

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:05.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
is we're the 28th
largest state population,

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:08.074
19th largest land-area wise.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.509
We've got some of
the cleanest water,

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.044
cleanest air in the country.

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.113 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have an all-of-the-above
approach in energy.

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:15.915
In other words, we
love oil and gas.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.617
We're number six
in oil production,

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.386
number four in natural gas,

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:22.522
but we're also number two
in wind energy production.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:26.126
So we're one of
only four states,

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:29.729
four states that 40% of our
energy comes from renewables.

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:34.067
And so it's very attractive to
a lot of companies right now.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.436
(soft music)

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.409 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and as you guys know,

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:44.377 line:0%
I'm off the grid
for all of August,

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.380 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
but we pre-taped a bunch
of interviews for you

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.783
before I left the internet.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:52.919
And joining me today is
the governor of Oklahoma,

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:56.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
Governor Kevin Stitt,
welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:57.991
- Hey, great to be with
you, thanks so much.

00:00:57.991 --> 00:01:00.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hope you're having a great
time on your vacation

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.561
when this is airing.

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.863 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I hope that I'm
having a great time too,

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.533 align:start size:84% position:16%
not paying attention
to the news,

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.301 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I am looking forward
to chatting with you

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.805
because we met a couple weeks
before I went off the grid.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:14.374
Governor DeSantis held a
little event in Florida

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:17.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
for some governors and
some Twitter personalities

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:19.212
or whatever the rest of us are.

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you brought up a
couple interesting points

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:22.649 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I immediately
went up to you after,

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:23.750
I said, "We gotta chat."

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.684
So here we are.

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:25.885
So before we do anything,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.288
you know, people
talk about Oklahoma

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:29.522
and most people probably say,

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.224 align:start size:81% position:19%
"Hey, what's going
on in Oklahoma?

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:33.460
I don't know much
about Oklahoma."

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.828
So first, why don't
we just get a little

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.329
of your personal history?

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:37.831 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then we'll dive
into some of the stuff

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.332
we need to know about Oklahoma.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:42.368 line:0%
- Absolutely. Well,
I'm the 28th governor

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.803 line:0%
of the great state of Oklahoma,

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.673 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
fourth generation Oklahoman,
and a business guy.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:50.944 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was never in politics
when I ran in 2018.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:53.413
My story, I grew up
in Norman, Oklahoma,

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.615 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the University
of Oklahoma is.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
My dad was a pastor
there and then-

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:00.920
But I went to Oklahoma state
in Stillwater for college,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.689
got an Accounting degree,

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:05.525 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then started my company
shortly after college

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.727 align:start size:84% position:16%
with a thousand
dollars and a computer

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.395 align:start size:84% position:16%
and lived the
American dream story.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:12.165
Today we have 1,600 employees,

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:14.100
do business all
over the country.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.202
And I got inspired to run,

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.638 align:start size:88% position:13%
I watched a
businessman get elected

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.640
President of the United States,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.743
and I saw my state
kind of struggling

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I looked and
saw who was running,

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:27.347
it was just more of
the same, really,

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:28.615
just career politicians.

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.750 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I said, "Hey, if
anything's gonna change,

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.118
we need business people.

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.620
We need a fresh set of eyes.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.389
We need people like our
founding fathers envision

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.358
that were gonna leave
the private sector,

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:40.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
go serve their country
or their state,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.062
and actually change
things and focus

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:45.932
on the next generation,
not the next election."

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.868 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I told that to
Oklahomans when I campaigned,

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.336
they agreed with me.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.572
We won the election and today,

00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:55.608
Oklahoma is really
exploding in growth.

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
We have so many
great things going.

00:02:57.744 --> 00:03:00.346 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've got the largest savings
account we've ever had,

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:04.083 align:start position:0%
we have the lowest unemployment,
I cut taxes last year,

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:07.053
more people are moving to
Oklahoma than ever before.

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:08.821
We're top 10 in the
country in that.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.424
And so things are just
really on the up and up

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.692
for our state.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.961
We're the 28th largest
state population-wise,

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:17.430
right above Texas figuratively

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.234
and actually right above Texas,
so I like to point that out.

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:24.370
- So I think a lot of
people watching this

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
are probably going,
"Wait a minute, Oklahoma?

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:28.174
It can't have that many problems

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
that a successful
business guy has to run."

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.679
I mean, we talk about
California's problems,

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:34.681
we talk about New
York's problems,

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:35.949
maybe Illinois's
problems, I mean,

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:38.051
mostly blue state problems.

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:40.386
What could possibly be
going on in Oklahoma

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.854
that a successful guy,

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:44.090 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, you were running
multimillion dollar business,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.092
would have to throw
his hat in the ring?

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:47.827
- Well, what I'm talking about,

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:50.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
we had a teacher
walkout, for example.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
We weren't paying our
teachers what market was.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I gave our
teachers a pay raise

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:56.069
to bring 'em up to market.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.205
Government won't fix itself,

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.241 align:start size:84% position:16%
government always
wants to spend more.

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:03.443
So bringing that
fiscal discipline,

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:06.946 align:start position:0%
I believe in smaller government,
I believe in lower taxes.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:10.483
So bringing some of
those disciplines
into our state budget

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:13.620 align:start size:84% position:16%
and running government
like a business,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.588
is kind of what I focused on.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.291
There's 33,000 state
employees in Oklahoma.

00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:21.060
It's a $22 billion budget.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.298 align:start size:88% position:13%
And normally people
hire non-business people

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:27.333
and people that have
never signed paychecks,

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.869
never set a vision, and
hired the right people,

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:31.404
held people accountable.

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:33.906
So those are all the things
that we brought to Oklahoma.

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:37.543
And so things are
moving the needle,

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:39.746
we've deregulated our business,

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:41.114
setting a level playing field,

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:42.749
not picking winners and losers.

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:46.085
So people understand, you know,

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.854
the great things about
moving to our state.

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:50.590 align:start size:84% position:16%
Energy policy, we have
an all-of-the-above
energy approach,

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:54.027 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's led to the
lowest cost of electricity

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:55.495
to a business or consumer.

00:04:55.495 --> 00:04:59.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
So keeping those
conservative values going,

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.933
even in a red state like ours,

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:03.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
sometimes the bureaucracy
and the government

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:04.270
can get away from you,

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.039
and you've gotta come
in with an outsider

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:08.574
to bring it back in check.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:10.710 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I heard you talk a bit
about this a couple weeks ago,

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.780
but how did you deal with
all the lockdown madness,

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.349
and figuring out what
to do and allowing,

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:19.786 align:start size:97% position:3%
or not allowing people to
live their lives accordingly?

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:21.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, you know,
first off, we believe

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:23.156
in freedoms in Oklahoma.

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:24.657
And we believe in
our Constitution,

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.160
and we're not gonna put our
Constitution in the attic,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
just because somebody
in Washington DC

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:31.464
says that we should do that.

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:34.967
You know? So we kept our
state open the entire time.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:36.803
I kept our schools open.

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:41.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wasn't going to say
this business is essential

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:42.408
and this business
is not essential.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:44.544 align:start size:84% position:16%
The only non-essential
business is one

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:45.812
that you didn't put capital in,

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:47.513
or you don't get
your paycheck from.

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:50.650
So picking winners
and losers like that

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:53.486 align:start size:84% position:16%
to me is un-American,
it's not right,

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:55.555
and what we've done
to our businesses,

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:57.390
and then what we did to our kids

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.926 align:start size:97% position:3%
by keeping 'em outta school
in these other states,

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:01.427
it's an atrocity.

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:05.465
So in Oklahoma, we
kept everything open.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:07.667
And because of that now,

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:10.570
people are realizing and
recognizing the benefits,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.972
our businesses are having
their best years ever,

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:14.774
and companies from California

00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:17.310
are moving to states like ours,

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.213 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they're
tired of the mandates

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:23.149
and the dictating from
the holier-than-thou

00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:25.785
government officials
that we know best,

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:27.954
and do as we say.

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:30.690 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So I think everyone
watching knows my story.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.558
And you heard it at
that event we did.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:35.928
I am a new Floridian, about
seven months in right now,

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:37.430
after living in California.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you mentioned you guys
are getting this influx

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:41.100
of people and businesses.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.368 line:0%
Does that worry you ever?

00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:43.970 line:0%
That you know, these
businesses come in,

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.138 line:0%
they bring a whole
bunch of people,

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:46.572 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
they've often voted
the wrong way,

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
which caused the problems
in the first place,

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:51.010 align:start size:94% position:6%
and now they have to
integrate into a new state

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.845
that is basically
doing it right?

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:55.515
- You know, we hear that a lot.

00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:58.518 align:start size:94% position:6%
The folks that we meet
from California, you know,

00:06:58.518 --> 00:06:59.986 align:start size:88% position:13%
I have those
conversations with 'em.

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:02.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
I jokingly tell 'em, "Hey,
I had to wait 18 years

00:07:02.054 --> 00:07:04.690
to vote in Oklahoma, so I
expect you to wait 18 years

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:05.892
before you can vote."

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:09.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
But they say, they go, "Hey,
listen, here's the deal.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:12.598
There's 12 million
people in California

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.167
that voted for president Trump.

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:17.870 align:start position:0%
The people that are leaving
are recognizing the differences

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.605 align:start size:84% position:16%
between a blue state
and a red state."

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:23.409 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so trust me, they're
saying, "We are on your side.

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:25.978 align:start size:97% position:3%
We believe in freedoms. We
believe in smaller government.

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.014
We believe in less regulation."

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:31.417 align:start size:97% position:3%
Doing business in California
is an absolute beat down.

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:34.954
I know that personally, and
from the regulatory climate,

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:37.156
from the taxation climate,

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:41.027
they're just so outta touch
with what our values are

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:43.129
as Oklahomans and
most of Americans

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I think normal
people are leaving there.

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:48.468
- Before we jump into
some of the specifics,

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:50.970
what else do people have
to know about Oklahoma?

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.672 align:start size:88% position:13%
I feel like it's just
like one of those states

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:54.373
that people kind of,
they go through it,

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:55.675
they maybe fly over it,

00:07:55.675 --> 00:07:57.243
and they don't think
about it that much,

00:07:57.243 --> 00:07:58.511
but you guys are
doing an awful lot

00:07:58.511 --> 00:08:00.346
when it comes to energy
and a whole bunch more.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:03.549
- Yeah. Well, I mean, I was
just in Farmborough, England,

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:04.750
representing our state

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.285 align:start size:84% position:16%
for the aerospace
and defense industry.

00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:09.355
And some of the things that
I like to remind people is,

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:11.691 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're the 28th largest
state population,

00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:14.460
19th largest land area-wise.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:15.895
We've got some of
the cleanest water,

00:08:15.895 --> 00:08:17.430
cleanest air in the country.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.532 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have an all-of-the-above
approach and energy,

00:08:19.532 --> 00:08:22.335
in other words, we
love oil and gas.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:24.003
We're number six
in oil production,

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:25.771
number four in natural gas,

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:28.808
but we're also number two
in wind energy production.

00:08:28.808 --> 00:08:32.512
So we're one of
only four states,

00:08:32.512 --> 00:08:36.082
four states that 40% of our
energy comes from renewables.

00:08:36.082 --> 00:08:40.686
And so it's very attractive to
a lot of companies right now,

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:43.923 align:start position:0%
but we have the largest
maintenance and repair facility

00:08:43.923 --> 00:08:46.459
on aviation in the country,
with Tinker Air Force Base,

00:08:46.459 --> 00:08:49.462
so great aviation industry.

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:50.863
We've got an inland port.

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:53.132 align:start size:97% position:3%
So most people don't
realize that about Oklahoma.

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:56.035
We have the farthest frost
free port in the country,

00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:58.504
all the way almost to Tulsa.

00:08:58.504 --> 00:09:01.574 align:start position:0%
So a lot of great things going,
we got great agriculture,

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:03.609
we feed the world
in a lot of cases

00:09:03.609 --> 00:09:05.645
from our agricultural side,

00:09:05.645 --> 00:09:08.548 align:start size:88% position:13%
number two beef
producer in the country.

00:09:08.548 --> 00:09:09.815
So a lot of great things going.

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:11.250
And then the freedoms.

00:09:12.418 --> 00:09:14.687
Oklahoma City, I think
is the 25th largest city

00:09:14.687 --> 00:09:16.188
in the country.

00:09:16.188 --> 00:09:18.090
We've got the funder of
professional sports team.

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.458
So the quality of life,

00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:22.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
the commute in Oklahoma
is like 15 minutes

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:25.798
compared to an hour in some of
the other major metro areas.

00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:27.567
So people love the
quality of life.

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:30.069
You gotta come visit Oklahoma.

00:09:30.069 --> 00:09:31.637
- And I'm guessing house prices

00:09:31.637 --> 00:09:35.241
a little bit better than
here in Florida right now.

00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:36.809
- Definitely right.

00:09:36.809 --> 00:09:38.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, we obviously
are, you know,

00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:40.947
everything's going up
all over the country,

00:09:40.947 --> 00:09:43.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we've got an
arbitrage right now.

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:44.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's what
businesses are seeing,

00:09:44.984 --> 00:09:46.319
is that they can come in

00:09:46.319 --> 00:09:49.322
and the quality of life
with the cost of living,

00:09:49.322 --> 00:09:53.292
allows them to pay less than
you can on some of the coast.

00:09:53.292 --> 00:09:56.729 align:start size:97% position:3%
And with COVID, you're seeing
people move and saying,

00:09:56.729 --> 00:09:59.298 align:start size:84% position:16%
"I don't have to work
in this big city,

00:09:59.298 --> 00:10:01.968
or through technology
we can live anywhere,"

00:10:01.968 --> 00:10:04.070 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we're also seeing
headquarters move

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:05.771
to Dallas, Fort Worth,

00:10:05.771 --> 00:10:08.874
and move to Oklahoma City
and move to other states

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:11.510
and get out of the
New York City areas

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
or the bay areas
where crime is rampant

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.015
and homelessness
and all the stuff

00:10:16.015 --> 00:10:19.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
that doesn't happen
in our state.

00:10:19.852 --> 00:10:22.188
- You mentioned the amount of
oil and gas you guys produce,

00:10:22.188 --> 00:10:24.457
and then also wind power.

00:10:24.457 --> 00:10:27.093
Does that cause any tension
in the business community

00:10:27.093 --> 00:10:28.894 align:start size:88% position:13%
or any tension with
the federal government?

00:10:28.894 --> 00:10:31.097
Because the federal
government at least,

00:10:31.097 --> 00:10:33.265
seems to view oil
and gas versus wind

00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:35.368
as very, very different things.

00:10:35.368 --> 00:10:37.470
They like one, they
don't like the other.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, that's the
thing that blows their mind

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:42.074
whenever I go and meet with
the Biden administration

00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:43.776
and their secretary of energy,

00:10:43.776 --> 00:10:45.044
and we talk about
it all the time.

00:10:45.044 --> 00:10:46.846
Those things can
coexist together.

00:10:46.846 --> 00:10:48.881 align:start size:84% position:16%
We believe in the free
market in Oklahoma,

00:10:48.881 --> 00:10:50.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
we believe in an
all-of-the-above approach.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.252
So they do coexist just fine.

00:10:53.252 --> 00:10:55.287
We love our oil
and gas industry,

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:58.658
and you can't have
an intellectually
honest conversation

00:10:58.658 --> 00:11:02.928
about the needs of Americans
and a reliable energy grid

00:11:02.928 --> 00:11:07.099
without talking about natural
gas and talking about oil,

00:11:07.099 --> 00:11:08.300
but we don't shy away.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:10.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're leaning into
hydrogen, for example.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:15.307 align:start size:88% position:13%
I signed an MOU
with the governor of
Arkansas and Louisiana,

00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:18.544
we believe that we will get
one of the four hydrogen hubs

00:11:18.544 --> 00:11:22.515
to prove out it's a $2
billion kind of program

00:11:22.515 --> 00:11:25.584 align:start position:0%
to prove out a commercialization
use of hydrogen,

00:11:25.584 --> 00:11:28.854 align:start position:0%
but you're gonna need pipelines
for hydrogen development.

00:11:28.854 --> 00:11:31.924
We can coexist together, and
you can talk about transition,

00:11:31.924 --> 00:11:34.060
and you can talk
about oil and gas.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.863
And again, we don't know
why there's such an attack

00:11:37.863 --> 00:11:40.366
on the oil and gas industry
from this administration,

00:11:40.366 --> 00:11:42.601 align:start size:81% position:19%
because what's
their alternative?

00:11:42.601 --> 00:11:46.105 align:start size:88% position:13%
Is to buy oil from
Russia or Saudi Arabia,

00:11:46.105 --> 00:11:48.374
because demand is the same.

00:11:48.374 --> 00:11:51.010
And here's something that I'd
like to point out to people.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:54.447 align:start size:94% position:6%
Every president of the
United States, since 1973,

00:11:54.447 --> 00:11:56.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
has had an energy
independence policy,

00:11:56.749 --> 00:11:58.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether they were
Republican or Democrat

00:11:58.951 --> 00:12:00.586
until this administration.

00:12:00.586 --> 00:12:03.022 align:start size:88% position:13%
And they wanted to
just cancel everything,

00:12:03.022 --> 00:12:07.226 align:start size:97% position:3%
cancel the Keystone Pipeline,
make drilling impossible,

00:12:07.226 --> 00:12:11.497
but what they forget is, we
still have to drive to work,

00:12:11.497 --> 00:12:13.132 align:start size:84% position:16%
we have to heat our
homes, our businesses,

00:12:13.132 --> 00:12:15.434 align:start size:88% position:13%
we have to drive our
kids to soccer practice,

00:12:15.434 --> 00:12:16.602
and football practice.

00:12:16.602 --> 00:12:18.304
And so the demand is the same.

00:12:18.304 --> 00:12:20.673
And they're not being honest.

00:12:20.673 --> 00:12:21.941
- Do you think they-

00:12:21.941 --> 00:12:23.542
Yeah, well that I
think is the part.

00:12:23.542 --> 00:12:25.711
Do you think that they actually

00:12:25.711 --> 00:12:27.179
are just simply not being honest

00:12:27.179 --> 00:12:29.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
or this is by design that
they know people have

00:12:29.448 --> 00:12:32.251 align:start size:97% position:3%
to go to work, they know
people have to get in the car,

00:12:32.251 --> 00:12:33.719
but they are sort of telling us,

00:12:33.719 --> 00:12:35.788
"Hey, we want off this stuff

00:12:35.788 --> 00:12:37.823
regardless of the
repercussions."

00:12:37.823 --> 00:12:40.292
- You know, I hope
that's not the case,

00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:42.328 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I've seen the
reports just like you

00:12:42.328 --> 00:12:44.597
that they make these
statements like, "Well,

00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:47.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
we knew that prices were gonna
be jacked up for a while,

00:12:47.333 --> 00:12:50.302
but that's gonna be
part of the process

00:12:50.302 --> 00:12:52.772
to get off of oil and gas."

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:55.241
And I hope that's not true,

00:12:55.241 --> 00:13:00.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
because to harm Oklahomans
or to harm Americans,

00:13:00.079 --> 00:13:03.449
just because you have some
kind of left-wing agenda,

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:06.886 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's not the president
that I would hope

00:13:06.886 --> 00:13:08.821 align:start size:84% position:16%
would be representing
our country.

00:13:08.821 --> 00:13:11.657
But, you know, I don't
know what's happening,

00:13:11.657 --> 00:13:15.327 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it doesn't make
common sense to most Americans

00:13:15.327 --> 00:13:18.464
on why they're burying
their head in the sand

00:13:18.464 --> 00:13:20.833
and not having an
honest conversation

00:13:20.833 --> 00:13:23.502
when just a couple years ago,
we were energy independent.

00:13:23.502 --> 00:13:26.605
And you're now seeing
inflation skyrocket,

00:13:26.605 --> 00:13:29.909 align:start size:97% position:3%
everything costs more,
40-year highs at everything.

00:13:29.909 --> 00:13:32.511
And actually they're
harming the people

00:13:32.511 --> 00:13:34.747
that they actually think
that they're protecting.

00:13:34.747 --> 00:13:36.482
That's what people
need to understand,

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:39.752
is these policies from
the Democratic party,

00:13:39.752 --> 00:13:44.690 align:start position:0%
actually hurt average
Oklahoma, middle-income people.

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:47.693
The rich get richer and it
hurts everyday Oklahomans.

00:13:47.693 --> 00:13:49.829
It's the Republicans,
it's a free market,

00:13:49.829 --> 00:13:53.432
it's the innovation of business

00:13:53.432 --> 00:13:55.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
that actually helps
the middle class grow.

00:13:57.069 --> 00:13:58.604
- Yeah. Absolutely.

00:13:58.604 --> 00:14:00.506
You're preaching to
the choir over here.

00:14:00.506 --> 00:14:03.509
My audience gets it.

00:14:03.509 --> 00:14:05.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
Anything else we should
know broadly about Oklahoma

00:14:05.311 --> 00:14:06.745 align:start size:88% position:13%
before I wanna dive
into some of this stuff

00:14:06.745 --> 00:14:07.780
that I heard you talking about,

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:08.981
about what's going on

00:14:08.981 --> 00:14:10.382 align:start size:88% position:13%
with the Native American
reservations there,

00:14:10.382 --> 00:14:11.951
which is really fascinating,

00:14:11.951 --> 00:14:13.886
but anything else you wanna
get off your chest quick?

00:14:13.886 --> 00:14:15.020
- Yeah, no, I'm good, I'm good.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.222
- You're good?
- I'll probably

00:14:16.222 --> 00:14:17.890
be circling back to it.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.793
- Okay. So when I heard you
speak a couple weeks ago,

00:14:20.793 --> 00:14:22.628
you told a really
fascinating story

00:14:22.628 --> 00:14:26.465
and was describing
some of the tension

00:14:26.465 --> 00:14:28.801
that Oklahoma faces
over what's going on

00:14:28.801 --> 00:14:31.103 align:start size:88% position:13%
with the Native American
reservations there,

00:14:31.103 --> 00:14:34.773 align:start size:94% position:6%
and how there is seemingly
a disparate distance

00:14:34.773 --> 00:14:37.343
in the way laws
are being applied

00:14:37.343 --> 00:14:39.445 align:start size:88% position:13%
when it comes to Native
American population

00:14:39.445 --> 00:14:41.814
versus the average Oklahoman.

00:14:41.814 --> 00:14:43.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
You told a couple
anecdotes about that,

00:14:43.148 --> 00:14:44.984
can you just kind of
gimme the 101 on that?

00:14:44.984 --> 00:14:46.185
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:46.185 --> 00:14:48.621 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, first off, the
McGirt decision came out

00:14:48.621 --> 00:14:51.523
in July of 2020, and
basically what it said,

00:14:51.523 --> 00:14:54.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was the US Supreme Court
decision called McGirt.

00:14:54.026 --> 00:14:55.561 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I'll encourage
everybody to look it up,

00:14:55.561 --> 00:14:58.864
but basically it said that
we still had reservations

00:14:58.864 --> 00:15:00.366
in Oklahoma.

00:15:00.366 --> 00:15:02.768 align:start size:97% position:3%
So about half of our state was
all of a sudden turned back

00:15:02.768 --> 00:15:04.436
into an Indian reservation.

00:15:04.436 --> 00:15:06.639 align:start size:84% position:16%
Tulsa, Oklahoma, with
a million people,

00:15:06.639 --> 00:15:08.707
was now considered an
Indian reservation.

00:15:08.707 --> 00:15:10.910
And here's the deal, I'm
not against the Indians.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:12.778 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm actually a member
of the Cherokee.

00:15:12.778 --> 00:15:14.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're proud of our heritage.
- Right.

00:15:14.847 --> 00:15:16.115
You literally, you yourself.

00:15:16.115 --> 00:15:18.150
- Literally, I can
show you my card.

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:20.386 align:start size:81% position:19%
My heritage, I
have Indian blood.

00:15:20.386 --> 00:15:23.355 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I have a membership
to the Indian,

00:15:23.355 --> 00:15:24.723
to the Cherokee Nation.

00:15:24.723 --> 00:15:26.892 align:start size:81% position:19%
So it's not an
anti-Indian thing,

00:15:26.892 --> 00:15:29.228 align:start size:84% position:16%
but this is a
pro-Oklahoma thing,

00:15:29.228 --> 00:15:30.763
this is a common sense thing.

00:15:30.763 --> 00:15:33.699
So what was happening is we
had tribal governments then

00:15:33.699 --> 00:15:35.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
saying that they
didn't have to follow

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.603 align:start size:81% position:19%
the laws of the
state of Oklahoma.

00:15:37.603 --> 00:15:39.905
They were saying that, no,
that if you were Indian,

00:15:39.905 --> 00:15:41.874 align:start size:84% position:16%
you didn't have to
pay taxes in Oklahoma,

00:15:41.874 --> 00:15:43.809
but every other race did.

00:15:43.809 --> 00:15:45.644
They were saying that
the district attorneys

00:15:45.644 --> 00:15:47.579
couldn't prosecute
crimes the same way.

00:15:47.579 --> 00:15:50.849
So you had a situation that
you didn't have fairness,

00:15:50.849 --> 00:15:53.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
you didn't have equal
protection under the law.

00:15:53.185 --> 00:15:57.589
And so I fought like crazy
against all odds, really,

00:15:57.589 --> 00:15:59.491
because they're super powerful.

00:15:59.491 --> 00:16:03.262
They're using taxpayer dollars
to get in and influence races

00:16:03.262 --> 00:16:05.030 align:start size:88% position:13%
and try to run negative
ads against you,

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:06.498
et cetera, et cetera.

00:16:06.498 --> 00:16:08.534
But I got a case back to
the Supreme Court and said,

00:16:08.534 --> 00:16:11.070
"Listen, we have to
be able to prosecute,

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.539
to protect public safety,

00:16:13.539 --> 00:16:15.641
all crimes in Eastern Oklahoma."

00:16:15.641 --> 00:16:17.977
And Castro-Huerta
case just came out

00:16:17.977 --> 00:16:21.714
since we talked, I believe,
that gave us a huge win.

00:16:21.714 --> 00:16:23.315
And they said, "Yes,

00:16:23.315 --> 00:16:27.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
you do have the right
to prosecute all
crimes in Oklahoma."

00:16:27.386 --> 00:16:29.521
And furthermore, they said
that Indian reservations

00:16:29.521 --> 00:16:32.291
are still part of a
state and therefore

00:16:32.291 --> 00:16:34.493
they're subject to
state jurisdiction,

00:16:34.493 --> 00:16:38.197
which gives us a lot
more certainty now

00:16:38.197 --> 00:16:39.932
than we had once before.

00:16:39.932 --> 00:16:42.101
So those are all
things that I think

00:16:42.101 --> 00:16:43.569
Americans need to be aware of,

00:16:43.569 --> 00:16:46.138
because this would be
like the Supreme Court

00:16:46.138 --> 00:16:48.073
coming into your city and
your state and saying,

00:16:48.073 --> 00:16:50.376
"Hey, this used to
be Indian land."

00:16:50.376 --> 00:16:53.545 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so you no longer
have to follow,

00:16:53.545 --> 00:16:56.548
they don't have to follow
zoning or taxation laws,

00:16:56.548 --> 00:16:58.050
you know, those kind of things.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:01.687
That's how it threw us into
kind of turmoil in our state.

00:17:01.687 --> 00:17:04.056 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Yeah. Well, I would imagine
that the average citizen,

00:17:04.056 --> 00:17:06.692 line:0%
after the McGirt
decision, probably,

00:17:06.692 --> 00:17:09.128
it increased tension actually

00:17:09.128 --> 00:17:10.396
between the communities, right?

00:17:10.396 --> 00:17:12.898
I mean, if you're just
the law abiding citizen

00:17:12.898 --> 00:17:14.733 align:start size:84% position:16%
and now someone
breaks into your house

00:17:14.733 --> 00:17:16.001
or whatever it might be,

00:17:16.001 --> 00:17:17.169
and then they're not
gonna be prosecuted

00:17:17.169 --> 00:17:18.504
because of their lineage,

00:17:18.504 --> 00:17:21.306
I could see some
tension developing.

00:17:21.306 --> 00:17:23.942 align:start size:97% position:3%
- A hundred percent. I mean,
we had district attorneys

00:17:23.942 --> 00:17:27.146 align:start position:0%
telling people that if you
have an Indian tag on your car,

00:17:27.146 --> 00:17:28.614
you need to park in the garage,

00:17:28.614 --> 00:17:31.784
because there were people
targeting those people

00:17:31.784 --> 00:17:33.085
because they knew that the state

00:17:33.085 --> 00:17:35.254
couldn't prosecute those crimes.

00:17:35.254 --> 00:17:36.722
So it was just ridiculous.

00:17:36.722 --> 00:17:40.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, the 911 calls
going into our 911 system,

00:17:40.359 --> 00:17:41.760
they were having to
ask the question,

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.128
"Well, are you native or not?"

00:17:43.128 --> 00:17:44.396
And people were like, "Well,

00:17:44.396 --> 00:17:45.531
do I have to tell you
if I'm native or not?

00:17:45.531 --> 00:17:46.665
'Cause then you won't come out."

00:17:46.665 --> 00:17:49.501
And so it was all this turmoil

00:17:49.501 --> 00:17:52.838 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm glad that the Supreme
Court did the right thing

00:17:52.838 --> 00:17:57.009
to say, "Hey, this
is still a state.

00:17:57.009 --> 00:17:59.378 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have to be on equal footing
with every other state.

00:17:59.378 --> 00:18:01.313
We have to be able
to regulate business

00:18:01.313 --> 00:18:04.883
and oil, and gas, and
zoning, and mining,

00:18:04.883 --> 00:18:07.186
and all the things
we can't turn that on

00:18:07.186 --> 00:18:11.223
to county by county,
depending on, you know,

00:18:11.223 --> 00:18:14.293
what Indian tribe used
to be located there.

00:18:14.293 --> 00:18:16.528
- How was that for you,
with your background

00:18:16.528 --> 00:18:18.597
having to go in and do
something that I'm sure

00:18:18.597 --> 00:18:20.032
a certain amount
of people thought

00:18:20.032 --> 00:18:22.701 align:start size:97% position:3%
you were a turn code or
there's probably words for it

00:18:22.701 --> 00:18:24.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we need not
mention on this show?

00:18:24.837 --> 00:18:26.638 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah. You know, here's
what I tell people.

00:18:26.638 --> 00:18:29.408
I said 95% of Native Americans

00:18:29.408 --> 00:18:32.344
like me that have part native
blood, we're proud of that,

00:18:32.344 --> 00:18:34.546
but we're American and
we're Oklahoma first.

00:18:34.546 --> 00:18:36.415
So they actually agreed with me.

00:18:36.415 --> 00:18:40.486
It was only those at the
very top of the tribe,

00:18:40.486 --> 00:18:43.088 align:start size:88% position:13%
the big bosses that
were really fighting us

00:18:43.088 --> 00:18:44.423
and putting money against us.

00:18:44.423 --> 00:18:47.025 align:start size:84% position:16%
One tribe said they
were gonna stop flying

00:18:47.025 --> 00:18:50.095
the Oklahoma flag on their land,

00:18:50.095 --> 00:18:52.598 align:start size:88% position:13%
which then the citizens
really revolted,

00:18:52.598 --> 00:18:54.299
and he quickly changed his tune.

00:18:54.299 --> 00:18:56.702
That chief did like two
days later and said,

00:18:56.702 --> 00:18:58.003
"Oh, okay, well I decided

00:18:58.003 --> 00:18:59.872
I would keep flying
the Oklahoma flag."

00:18:59.872 --> 00:19:01.974
So they took it too far.

00:19:01.974 --> 00:19:04.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, you know, things
have a tendency

00:19:04.476 --> 00:19:06.778
if pendulum swings too far,

00:19:06.778 --> 00:19:08.780
people go, "Hey, this
doesn't feel right."

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:10.582
And it comes kind
of back to balance

00:19:10.582 --> 00:19:13.051
and we believe that's
why the Supreme Court

00:19:13.051 --> 00:19:15.053
brought that case back.

00:19:15.053 --> 00:19:16.755
- Let me ask you as
a Native American,

00:19:16.755 --> 00:19:18.857 align:start size:88% position:13%
that means automatically
you have to represent

00:19:18.857 --> 00:19:20.025
all Native Americans.

00:19:20.025 --> 00:19:21.994
How do you feel about, you know,

00:19:21.994 --> 00:19:24.196 align:start size:81% position:19%
when they have
the Atlanta Braves

00:19:24.196 --> 00:19:26.031
or the Washington Redskins?

00:19:26.031 --> 00:19:27.766
You know, it usually
seems like it's a bit

00:19:27.766 --> 00:19:29.001
of a media construct,

00:19:29.001 --> 00:19:30.903
but obviously everyone
has their own feelings.

00:19:30.903 --> 00:19:33.906 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, listen,
we're so sensitive.

00:19:33.906 --> 00:19:35.307
It doesn't bother me a bit.

00:19:35.307 --> 00:19:36.975
Every single person like me,

00:19:36.975 --> 00:19:40.479
10% of Oklahoma's population
are native, about 400,000.

00:19:40.479 --> 00:19:44.983 align:start size:97% position:3%
And, you know, I think the
media blows out of proportion.

00:19:44.983 --> 00:19:48.754
It's just like anything, it's
a very small vocal minority,

00:19:48.754 --> 00:19:51.056 align:start size:88% position:13%
and what's disappointing
is these businesses

00:19:51.056 --> 00:19:54.660
and these professional
teams just end up caving,

00:19:55.928 --> 00:19:59.498
for, you know, a very
small vocal minority.

00:19:59.498 --> 00:20:01.733
And I know that's kind
of where we're at today,

00:20:01.733 --> 00:20:04.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I think the
bigger point to me is

00:20:05.437 --> 00:20:07.005
we believe in America

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:10.509 align:start size:88% position:13%
and based on your merit,
and your hard work,

00:20:10.509 --> 00:20:14.146
and your education, and
how hard you wanna work

00:20:14.146 --> 00:20:15.681
depends on your success.

00:20:15.681 --> 00:20:18.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
Not the color of your
skin or your nationality.

00:20:18.383 --> 00:20:21.186
I want a system that
is fair for everybody

00:20:21.186 --> 00:20:23.021
and doesn't pick
winners and losers.

00:20:23.021 --> 00:20:26.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think when we
try to pay people off,

00:20:26.058 --> 00:20:29.194
or give money to this race,
or try to quote on quote,

00:20:29.194 --> 00:20:32.531
"Right this wrong", I think
those are bad precedents,

00:20:32.531 --> 00:20:34.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it'll end up
breaking our country

00:20:34.633 --> 00:20:36.301
and it divides us further.

00:20:37.402 --> 00:20:39.004
- Do you sense we're divided

00:20:39.004 --> 00:20:41.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
sort of in an
irreparable fashion?

00:20:41.173 --> 00:20:42.641
I mean, when you
talk about freedom

00:20:42.641 --> 00:20:44.843
and capitalism and
limited government

00:20:44.843 --> 00:20:46.111
and all those things that, yeah.

00:20:46.111 --> 00:20:48.413
That's happening in red
states, it really is.

00:20:48.413 --> 00:20:50.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
It seems to be strengthening
in certain states.

00:20:50.282 --> 00:20:52.517 align:start size:84% position:16%
Certainly the two
states that we're in,

00:20:52.517 --> 00:20:54.186 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's not happening
in blue states.

00:20:54.186 --> 00:20:55.988
And it doesn't seem like
it's gonna turn around

00:20:55.988 --> 00:20:57.522
in blue states.

00:20:57.522 --> 00:20:59.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
What do you think about
that tension going forward

00:20:59.258 --> 00:21:00.859
as the United States of America?

00:21:02.294 --> 00:21:04.029 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, I mean that's
the million dollar question.

00:21:04.029 --> 00:21:07.232
You know, you look
back to our leaders

00:21:07.232 --> 00:21:09.034
like Ronald Reagan and it felt

00:21:09.034 --> 00:21:10.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
like he could bring
people together

00:21:10.702 --> 00:21:12.571
and kind of talk
about capitalism

00:21:12.571 --> 00:21:15.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the exceptionalism
of America

00:21:15.073 --> 00:21:17.943
in a way that kind of
brought us together

00:21:17.943 --> 00:21:19.544
and revitalized our country.

00:21:19.544 --> 00:21:21.847 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we hope that
we can get back there,

00:21:21.847 --> 00:21:25.117
because I believe that
the majority of Americans

00:21:25.117 --> 00:21:26.652
think like I do.

00:21:26.652 --> 00:21:30.389
We believe in God.

00:21:30.389 --> 00:21:33.125
And I think you take
God out of the values,

00:21:33.125 --> 00:21:34.626
and we believe in-

00:21:34.626 --> 00:21:38.230
We were founded our country
on these biblical values,

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:41.199 align:start size:84% position:16%
we believe in
freedom of religion.

00:21:41.199 --> 00:21:43.068 align:start position:0%
That means we don't discriminate
against any religion,

00:21:43.068 --> 00:21:45.170 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we certainly
don't discriminate it.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:48.974
We can't discriminate against
the Christian faith either.

00:21:48.974 --> 00:21:52.544
And so I think that most
people believe in family,

00:21:52.544 --> 00:21:56.848
and capitalism, and
you have to work hard

00:21:56.848 --> 00:21:58.350
and let's create a
level playing field

00:21:58.350 --> 00:21:59.951
for everybody to go succeed.

00:21:59.951 --> 00:22:02.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's just disappointing
when some people think

00:22:02.187 --> 00:22:04.122
that the answer is
government handouts,

00:22:04.122 --> 00:22:06.358 align:start size:81% position:19%
and they think
that the answer is

00:22:06.358 --> 00:22:08.994
more government and more taxes.

00:22:08.994 --> 00:22:11.196
That's just, you can
look across history,

00:22:11.196 --> 00:22:14.433 align:start position:0%
you can look at other countries
that have tried that model,

00:22:14.433 --> 00:22:16.134
and I think that's failing.

00:22:16.134 --> 00:22:20.105
But I still believe that
the majority of Americans

00:22:20.105 --> 00:22:21.373
think like we do.

00:22:21.373 --> 00:22:24.443
So if we say this
in a correct way,

00:22:24.443 --> 00:22:27.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
I believe that's
the winning message.

00:22:27.813 --> 00:22:29.081
- Yeah. I'm with you.

00:22:29.081 --> 00:22:30.882
How have you guys
done when it comes

00:22:30.882 --> 00:22:32.384
to all of the woke stuff?

00:22:32.384 --> 00:22:35.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
Keeping the gender stuff and
the critical race theory,

00:22:35.887 --> 00:22:37.356
all of that out of the schools,

00:22:37.356 --> 00:22:40.058 align:start size:97% position:3%
or has it seeped in and
what are you doing about it?

00:22:40.058 --> 00:22:43.462 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, again, sometimes
it tries to seep in.

00:22:43.462 --> 00:22:45.330
What I've done about it is I,

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.766
as the first governor in the
country that passed the bill

00:22:47.766 --> 00:22:50.335
that bans critical race theory,

00:22:50.335 --> 00:22:53.438
and the media kind of calls
it critical race theory,

00:22:53.438 --> 00:22:54.706
but here's all my bill said.

00:22:54.706 --> 00:22:58.176 align:start size:88% position:13%
It just simply said that
in our school system,

00:22:58.176 --> 00:23:01.113 align:start size:88% position:13%
we're not gonna teach
that one race or one sex

00:23:01.113 --> 00:23:03.115
is superior to another
race or another sex.

00:23:03.115 --> 00:23:04.383
That's all it says.

00:23:04.383 --> 00:23:07.119
And I don't know how
you can argue, huh?

00:23:07.119 --> 00:23:08.353
- Old school.

00:23:08.353 --> 00:23:10.021 align:start size:88% position:13%
- The old school. How
can you argue with that?

00:23:10.021 --> 00:23:12.023
Let's teach our kids,
not indoctrinate 'em

00:23:12.023 --> 00:23:13.392
and teach 'em that
they're inferior

00:23:13.392 --> 00:23:14.526 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of the
color of their skin,

00:23:14.526 --> 00:23:15.927
or they're somehow responsible

00:23:15.927 --> 00:23:18.196 align:start size:97% position:3%
for something that happened
a hundred years ago.

00:23:18.196 --> 00:23:19.931 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm not gonna teach
that to a first grader.

00:23:19.931 --> 00:23:21.867
That's ridiculous.

00:23:21.867 --> 00:23:25.670
And so I also signed the
Save Women in Sport Bill.

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:28.006
Which to us in Oklahoma, I mean,

00:23:28.006 --> 00:23:29.908
we don't know what's going on
with the rest of the country,

00:23:29.908 --> 00:23:33.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
because for us, it's like
everybody applauds me

00:23:33.945 --> 00:23:36.281 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we're gonna
protect women's sports

00:23:36.281 --> 00:23:38.784
and athletics and girls are
gonna play in girls sports,

00:23:38.784 --> 00:23:40.819
and boys are gonna
play in boys sports.

00:23:40.819 --> 00:23:42.854
I'm not gonna let
a biological male

00:23:42.854 --> 00:23:45.791 align:start size:84% position:16%
compete in high school
against a female

00:23:45.791 --> 00:23:47.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's been training
her whole life

00:23:47.125 --> 00:23:50.061
where he has physical
advantages on the track

00:23:50.061 --> 00:23:53.198 align:start size:88% position:13%
or in the swimming
pool against that girl.

00:23:53.198 --> 00:23:55.467 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it doesn't make
sense to us in Oklahoma,

00:23:55.467 --> 00:23:58.270
so it's easy for me to
sign these kind of bills.

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:00.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Is there any pushback
on that stuff in Oklahoma?

00:24:00.672 --> 00:24:01.907
You get anything?

00:24:01.907 --> 00:24:04.910
- I mean, you get some,
the whacked out folks,

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:07.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's a very
small minority here.

00:24:09.014 --> 00:24:10.982
- Yeah. What else is going on

00:24:10.982 --> 00:24:12.884
since you're talking
so much about business,

00:24:12.884 --> 00:24:14.553
and that really is
your background,

00:24:14.553 --> 00:24:16.855
and you've mentioned this
sort of low, you know,

00:24:16.855 --> 00:24:18.290
less regulation and
that sort of thing.

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:20.091
What else is going on
in the business world?

00:24:20.091 --> 00:24:22.327
If someone's watching
this in Cali right now,

00:24:22.327 --> 00:24:23.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they're thinking
about getting out

00:24:23.795 --> 00:24:26.998 align:start position:0%
and I get emails every day from
Cali people on the way out,

00:24:26.998 --> 00:24:29.000 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I'm usually selling
them on Florida,

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:30.869
but if they wanna stop
before they get here

00:24:30.869 --> 00:24:32.437
and see what's going on?

00:24:32.437 --> 00:24:33.905
- You gotta stop.

00:24:33.905 --> 00:24:35.907 align:start position:0%
I mean, listen, Florida's second
place compared to Oklahoma.

00:24:35.907 --> 00:24:38.443
Okay? You might have a
little bit of the ocean,

00:24:38.443 --> 00:24:41.847 align:start size:94% position:6%
but if you wanna make
money and you wanna be...

00:24:41.847 --> 00:24:43.081
And you want the
quality of life,

00:24:43.081 --> 00:24:44.516
Oklahoma is the
right spot to be.

00:24:44.516 --> 00:24:47.686 align:start position:0%
But, you know, technology
companies were going hard after.

00:24:47.686 --> 00:24:50.589 align:start position:0%
I'm looking at where the
research and development dollars

00:24:50.589 --> 00:24:52.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
are headed, where
we just sent Atlanta

00:24:52.557 --> 00:24:55.494
a huge, huge electric
vehicle manufacturer

00:24:55.494 --> 00:24:58.029
were going after
that supply chain,

00:24:58.029 --> 00:24:59.731
around electric vehicles.

00:24:59.731 --> 00:25:02.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have more electric
vehicle charging stations

00:25:02.434 --> 00:25:04.369
per capita than any other state,

00:25:04.369 --> 00:25:06.071
level-three charging stations.

00:25:06.071 --> 00:25:07.472
So we're leaning
into that stuff.

00:25:07.472 --> 00:25:10.041 align:start size:97% position:3%
Most people go, "Well,
Oklahoma's just oil and gas,"

00:25:10.041 --> 00:25:10.976
simply not true.

00:25:10.976 --> 00:25:12.444
I mean, we love our oil and gas,

00:25:12.444 --> 00:25:15.347 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we are making sure
that we do our part

00:25:15.347 --> 00:25:17.249
for our country on
oil and gas side,

00:25:17.249 --> 00:25:19.918
but we're also not naive to

00:25:19.918 --> 00:25:21.586 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the R&amp;D
dollars are headed.

00:25:21.586 --> 00:25:23.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not in combustible
engines anymore,

00:25:23.321 --> 00:25:25.190
they're in electric vehicles.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.993
So we are all in there.

00:25:27.993 --> 00:25:29.227
We're going into hydrogen,

00:25:29.227 --> 00:25:32.364
we are looking at
that transition

00:25:32.364 --> 00:25:35.600 align:start size:97% position:3%
and what type of other
energy sources can we have.

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:38.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
And as California's
having rolling blackouts,

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:40.372 align:start size:84% position:16%
we have the cheapest
electricity costs

00:25:40.372 --> 00:25:42.374
to the business in the country.

00:25:43.742 --> 00:25:46.344 align:start position:0%
- Yeah. It's nice to hear things
are going right sometimes.

00:25:46.344 --> 00:25:47.913
Right? I mean, so what
are your headaches then?

00:25:47.913 --> 00:25:49.648 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, what are
really the headaches?

00:25:49.648 --> 00:25:51.783
You're giving me a pretty
rosy picture of state,

00:25:51.783 --> 00:25:54.152
and you seem like things
are pretty sensible there

00:25:54.152 --> 00:25:56.087 align:start size:97% position:3%
and all that stuff, you must
have a couple headaches.

00:25:56.087 --> 00:25:57.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, I've got a
couple teenage daughters.

00:25:57.722 --> 00:26:01.660
So if you could help me,
I've got six kids and...

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:03.094
- Six kids?

00:26:03.094 --> 00:26:05.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, my wife and I we've
been married 24 years.

00:26:05.263 --> 00:26:09.401
We have six children. Our
oldest is 21 down to eight.

00:26:09.401 --> 00:26:12.137 align:start size:81% position:19%
So just like every
business person

00:26:12.137 --> 00:26:15.407
or mom that's having to
work you have to balance

00:26:16.575 --> 00:26:18.710
your home life, and
raising your kids,

00:26:18.710 --> 00:26:21.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
and being a good husband,
and a good father

00:26:21.279 --> 00:26:22.981
as well as your day job,

00:26:22.981 --> 00:26:25.817
which my day job happens to
be governor of the state.

00:26:25.817 --> 00:26:28.420 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's always
busy, but it's fun.

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:29.955
It's an honorable lifetime

00:26:29.955 --> 00:26:33.425
to serve your state where
your grandparents grew up.

00:26:33.425 --> 00:26:35.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I pinched myself
thinking about my granddad

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:37.329 align:start size:88% position:13%
who had an
eighth-grade education.

00:26:37.329 --> 00:26:40.799
He was a dairy farmer
in Skiatook, Oklahoma,

00:26:40.799 --> 00:26:43.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
and now his grandson
is governor.

00:26:43.702 --> 00:26:46.371
So I just wanna work my tail off

00:26:46.371 --> 00:26:48.306
and make sure I leave my state

00:26:48.306 --> 00:26:51.076
the best possible
state it can be

00:26:51.076 --> 00:26:53.612
when I come back to
the private sector.

00:26:53.612 --> 00:26:55.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You couldn't give anything
other than the kids.

00:26:55.213 --> 00:26:56.414
So just, all right.

00:26:56.414 --> 00:26:57.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you're in a pretty
functioning state

00:26:57.682 --> 00:26:59.451
that seems like things
are going pretty well.

00:26:59.451 --> 00:27:01.453 align:start size:97% position:3%
The woke stuff's not getting
in, you got business,

00:27:01.453 --> 00:27:04.222 line:0%
you got natural resources,
and all of those things.

00:27:04.222 --> 00:27:06.591 line:0%
Was there anything when you
came in as a businessman

00:27:06.591 --> 00:27:09.160 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
to learn how government
operates and look at budgets,

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:10.662 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the way they spend
it, all that stuff,

00:27:10.662 --> 00:27:11.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
were you just pulling
your hair out?

00:27:11.963 --> 00:27:13.365
I'm trying to get something here

00:27:13.365 --> 00:27:15.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
that might have frustrated
you along the way.

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:17.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, I mean, I don't
really want my legislature

00:27:17.502 --> 00:27:18.803
to see this, but, you know,

00:27:18.803 --> 00:27:21.172 align:start size:97% position:3%
working with the
legislature's been difficult.

00:27:21.172 --> 00:27:24.743 align:start position:0%
You know, number one, I'm
trying to get school choice done

00:27:24.743 --> 00:27:26.244
across the finish line.

00:27:26.244 --> 00:27:29.180
I think that's the issue that
would turn our state around.

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:31.249
We want to empower parents,

00:27:31.249 --> 00:27:34.953
not just the zip code that a
kid is happen to be born in,

00:27:34.953 --> 00:27:35.987
or living at this time.

00:27:35.987 --> 00:27:37.622
And I tell people all the time,

00:27:37.622 --> 00:27:39.724
school choice should
not be for the rich,

00:27:39.724 --> 00:27:41.226
it should be for everyone.

00:27:41.226 --> 00:27:44.629
And these are kids in Oklahoma

00:27:44.629 --> 00:27:46.731
and there's 700,000 kids,

00:27:46.731 --> 00:27:49.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
let's make sure we educate
'em and we give them

00:27:49.034 --> 00:27:51.236
the best opportunity, we've
put parents back in charge.

00:27:51.236 --> 00:27:52.637
So trying to get that
across the finish line

00:27:52.637 --> 00:27:54.873
is a goal of mine if
I'm fortunate enough.

00:27:54.873 --> 00:27:56.241 align:start size:88% position:13%
- What kind of pushback
do you get on that?

00:27:56.241 --> 00:27:57.842
Because I would imagine
in a red state, I mean,

00:27:57.842 --> 00:27:59.778
there's so much momentum
behind that right now.

00:27:59.778 --> 00:28:01.246 align:start size:84% position:16%
I can't imagine it's
too much, right?

00:28:01.246 --> 00:28:03.448
- Well, we did not get
it across the finish line

00:28:03.448 --> 00:28:04.382
this past session.

00:28:04.382 --> 00:28:05.583
Hopefully, we can next year,

00:28:05.583 --> 00:28:08.953
but really the rural folks that-

00:28:08.953 --> 00:28:10.789
And they're right in the
fact that there aren't

00:28:10.789 --> 00:28:12.190
a lot of the private schools,

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:14.059 align:start size:97% position:3%
but those aren't the
schools we're talking about.

00:28:14.059 --> 00:28:15.860
A lot of it it's the inner city,

00:28:15.860 --> 00:28:17.562
it's the large school districts

00:28:17.562 --> 00:28:19.531 align:start size:88% position:13%
that are indoctrinating
the kids.

00:28:19.531 --> 00:28:20.799
And then you have to try to-

00:28:20.799 --> 00:28:22.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
You get parents
that are showing us

00:28:22.167 --> 00:28:23.868
the books that they're teaching.

00:28:23.868 --> 00:28:27.138 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can't micromanage every
single school district,

00:28:27.138 --> 00:28:30.442 align:start size:97% position:3%
so let's inject competition,
let parents choose

00:28:30.442 --> 00:28:31.876 align:start size:84% position:16%
where to send their
kids to school,

00:28:31.876 --> 00:28:33.778 align:start size:97% position:3%
whether it's a Christian
school, or a Catholic school,

00:28:33.778 --> 00:28:38.383
or a charter school, we need
more of that in our state.

00:28:38.383 --> 00:28:41.019
So that's definitely a challenge

00:28:41.019 --> 00:28:44.155
to try to get across the
finish line for our state.

00:28:44.155 --> 00:28:47.125
Budget process, it's
got its own challenges.

00:28:47.125 --> 00:28:50.495
I'm always trying to
hold our budgets flat.

00:28:50.495 --> 00:28:52.564
And sometimes you fight

00:28:53.865 --> 00:28:55.366
with some of the legislature

00:28:55.366 --> 00:28:59.637
that wants to grow government,
to be quite honest with you.

00:28:59.637 --> 00:29:03.141
And you know, you have to
really hold that in check

00:29:03.141 --> 00:29:04.642
because we're always...

00:29:04.642 --> 00:29:07.445 align:start size:88% position:13%
Nobody it seems like
fights for the taxpayer.

00:29:07.445 --> 00:29:09.581 align:start size:97% position:3%
You've got all these lobbyists
and all these industries

00:29:09.581 --> 00:29:10.815
will come in and hire folks

00:29:10.815 --> 00:29:12.784
that advocate for
their industry,

00:29:12.784 --> 00:29:14.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
but nobody advocates
for the taxpayer.

00:29:14.953 --> 00:29:16.387 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's my job,
that's what I tell 'em,

00:29:16.387 --> 00:29:18.857 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Hey, I'm here fighting
for the taxpayer.

00:29:18.857 --> 00:29:20.725
Let's hold budgets flat.

00:29:20.725 --> 00:29:23.595
Let's make sure that we give
money back to the taxpayer

00:29:23.595 --> 00:29:25.830
in the form of lower taxes."

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:27.532 align:start size:84% position:16%
- How much
coordination do you do

00:29:27.532 --> 00:29:28.900
with some other red
state governors?

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:30.135
'Cause the event
that we were at,

00:29:30.135 --> 00:29:31.636
it was kind of off
the record at first,

00:29:31.636 --> 00:29:33.338
but of course the media gets
it and then they announced

00:29:33.338 --> 00:29:34.973
everybody that was
there is what it is.

00:29:34.973 --> 00:29:37.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you know, there are
obviously a bunch of other

00:29:37.108 --> 00:29:38.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
red state governors
there, and it seemed like

00:29:38.877 --> 00:29:40.779
you guys kind of talked
some of this stuff out.

00:29:40.779 --> 00:29:42.046
I don't know, know
if it's official,

00:29:42.046 --> 00:29:43.782
but you know, really
go back and forth on

00:29:43.782 --> 00:29:45.550
it had a lot to do
with COVID lockdowns

00:29:45.550 --> 00:29:48.052 align:start size:88% position:13%
and how maybe you would
do it going forward

00:29:48.052 --> 00:29:49.888
if they tried to
pull anything else.

00:29:49.888 --> 00:29:52.423
But is there a lot
of coordination on
that kind of stuff

00:29:52.423 --> 00:29:53.525
or other issues?

00:29:53.525 --> 00:29:55.960
- You know, I think there is.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:58.062
I would call it a lot.

00:29:58.062 --> 00:29:59.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
Republican Governors
Association,

00:29:59.597 --> 00:30:01.900 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think is the
greatest organization

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:04.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
as far as political action
group in the country.

00:30:04.269 --> 00:30:05.937
So I meet with those
governors probably

00:30:05.937 --> 00:30:07.205
three or four times a year.

00:30:07.205 --> 00:30:08.773
We all have each
other's cellphones,

00:30:08.773 --> 00:30:12.177
so we'll text each other and
I'll call Billy in Tennessee,

00:30:12.177 --> 00:30:14.045
you know, for example and say,

00:30:14.045 --> 00:30:15.480 align:start size:81% position:19%
"Hey, what are you
doing on this?"

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:18.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or, "Hey, I saw something
in the paper about-

00:30:18.149 --> 00:30:19.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, Tennessee's
doing this.

00:30:19.951 --> 00:30:21.886
Can you walk me through
your thought process?"

00:30:21.886 --> 00:30:24.322
So we take ideas from
each other for sure.

00:30:24.322 --> 00:30:26.724
And then we bounce, you know,

00:30:26.724 --> 00:30:28.927
kind of what we're seeing in
our state with one another.

00:30:28.927 --> 00:30:31.062
So it's a great collegial group

00:30:31.062 --> 00:30:33.631
that we're able to meet
and kind of going through

00:30:33.631 --> 00:30:35.033
the same thing together.

00:30:35.033 --> 00:30:37.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
COVID was a gut
punch for all of us.

00:30:37.769 --> 00:30:41.606
So we were all getting
hammered from the media

00:30:41.606 --> 00:30:43.641 align:start size:88% position:13%
on, you know, demanding
it not locked down.

00:30:43.641 --> 00:30:45.677 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was one of the only
governors in the country

00:30:45.677 --> 00:30:48.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
that refused to do a mask
mandate, for example,

00:30:48.112 --> 00:30:52.183
statewide and just got
hammered by the media.

00:30:52.183 --> 00:30:53.818 align:start size:81% position:19%
So we've got a
liberal, you know,

00:30:53.818 --> 00:30:55.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
fake news media here
in Oklahoma too.

00:30:57.021 --> 00:30:58.857
- Ah, finally I got something.

00:30:58.857 --> 00:31:00.391
I got something.

00:31:00.391 --> 00:31:02.160
Okay. I don't know why that
gives me a little bit of joy.

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:03.595
It can't be that perfect there.

00:31:03.595 --> 00:31:05.396 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, I'm loving Florida
these days. All right.

00:31:05.396 --> 00:31:07.232
Well, you're gonna appreciate
what I'm gonna do right now

00:31:07.232 --> 00:31:10.535 align:start size:97% position:3%
because I am also a capitalist
and a free market guy.

00:31:10.535 --> 00:31:12.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'm gonna thank you for
joining me on the show,

00:31:12.804 --> 00:31:15.640
and then I'm gonna ask
you one more question,

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:17.609
perhaps the most
important question

00:31:17.609 --> 00:31:20.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
about Republican
leadership going forward.

00:31:20.411 --> 00:31:23.781 align:start position:0%
We're gonna do that exclusively
for our subscribers.

00:31:23.781 --> 00:31:25.149
So we're gonna wrap up this one

00:31:25.149 --> 00:31:26.751
and go right over to
the locals community.

00:31:26.751 --> 00:31:29.687
So governor Kevin Stitt, I
thank you for joining us.

00:31:29.687 --> 00:31:31.022
If you're looking
for more honest

00:31:31.022 --> 00:31:32.991 align:start size:97% position:3%
and thoughtful conversations
